cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
 Clause86: Article-86-Compliant Hiring Decisions via Graph Tsetlin Machines
 with Faithful Clause Explanations and Counterfactual Recourse
authors:
 - family-names: Anwar
  affiliation: University of Agder
year: 2026
abstract: >-
 EU AI Act Article 86 (effective 2 August 2026) grants any person affected
 by a high-risk AI decision the right to "clear and meaningful explanations
 of the role of the AI system in the decision-making procedure and the main
 elements of the decision taken." Post-hoc explainers (SHAP, LIME) are
 approximations the deployer cannot demonstrate are faithful to the model.
 Clause86 produces hiring decisions as a Graph Tsetlin Machine over
 subword-dependency graphs of resumes (and tabular features); the firing
 clauses ARE the main elements of the decision, by construction; a SAT
 solver verifies that the recorded clauses are consistent with the recorded
 label and that the offered counterfactual recourse is minimal; clause
 inspection lets the deployer delete rules that use protected proxies,
 closing the loop between Article 86 transparency and Article 14 effective
 oversight.
keywords:
 - Tsetlin Machine
 - Graph Tsetlin Machine
 - EU AI Act
 - Article 86
 - right to explanation
 - interpretable hiring
 - counterfactual recourse
 - algorithmic fairness
 - SAT verification
license: MIT
